issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**
The usb_dc_sam0 driver is leaking memory when the usb device is detached using usb_disable().

https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/120df9aa1be6ff41be775646ca789f0e8e5ef5d3/drivers/usb/device/usb_dc_sam0.c#L400-L412

When a device is detached, the code snippet above does not reliably deallocated descriptor bank memory.  Therefore, upon the next attach and configure, another block of memory is allocated.  After several connect / disconnect sequences, the usb interface becomes unresponsive.

**To Reproduce**
Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1.  Build application using usb console on sam0 board using usb_disable() to disable interface when usb is detached.
2. attach and detach usb cable several times.
3. console becomes unresponsive after several cycles.

**Expected behavior**
usb console remains responsive.

I developed a work around for this issue as follows:
```
diff --git a/drivers/usb/device/usb_dc_sam0.c b/drivers/usb/device/usb_dc_sam0.c
index d4c0c3a0e6..059080f127 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/device/usb_dc_sam0.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/device/usb_dc_sam0.c
@@ -288,6 +288,24 @@ int usb_dc_attach(void)
        return 0;
 }
 
+static int usb_dc_release_buffers(void)
+{
+       struct usb_sam0_data *data = usb_sam0_get_data();
+       UsbDeviceDescBank *bank;
+       void *buf;
+
+       /* release the buffers */
+       for (int i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(data->descriptors); i++) {
+               for ( int j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(data->descriptors[0].DeviceDescBank); j++) {
+                       bank = &data->descriptors[i].DeviceDescBank[j];
+                       buf = (void *)bank->ADDR.reg;
+                       k_free(buf);
+                       bank->ADDR.reg = NULL;
+               }
+       }
+
+}
+
 /* Detach from the bus */
 int usb_dc_detach(void)
 {
@@ -296,6 +314,8 @@ int usb_dc_detach(void)
        regs->CTRLB.bit.DETACH = 1;
        usb_sam0_wait_syncbusy();
 
+       usb_dc_release_buffers();
+
        return 0;
 }
```

review_comments:
Do you mean like this:
```
static int usb_dc_release_buffers(void)
{
	struct usb_sam0_data *data = usb_sam0_get_data();
	UsbDeviceDescBank *bank;
	uintptr_t buf;

	/* release the buffers */
	for (int i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(data->descriptors); i++) {
		for (int j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(data->descriptors[0].DeviceDescBank); j++) {
			bank = &data->descriptors[i].DeviceDescBank[j];
			buf = bank->ADDR.reg;
			k_free(buf);
			bank->ADDR.reg = (uintptr_t)NULL;
		}
	}

}
```

Your point also brings up another question I had considered but elected to not pursue because it touches other code.  That is, the memory should likely be deallocated when the ep is disabled in usb_dc_ep_disable().  The problem with this approach Is that the call to usb_disable() (subsys/usb/usb_device.c)  is asynchronous with the usb stack events and the stack will never receive an event when usb_disable() is called.  One way to address this would be to insert a call to usb_dc_ep_disable() from usb_disable() or to generate a DC_RESET event from usb_disable().

For example:

```
int usb_disable(void)
{
	int ret;

	if (usb_dev.enabled != true) {
		/*Already disabled*/
		return 0;
	}

	forward_status_cb(USB_DC_RESET, NULL);

	ret = usb_dc_detach();
	if (ret < 0) {
		return ret;
	}

	/* Disable VBUS if needed */
	usb_vbus_set(false);

	usb_dev.enabled = false;

	return 0;
}
```

and release the memory in usb_dc_ep_disable() where we can iterate over the endpoints.  Thoughts?

Further to my comment above about using the DC_RESET event, here is what usb_dc_ep_disable() would look like:
```
int usb_dc_ep_disable(uint8_t ep)
{
+	struct usb_sam0_data *data = usb_sam0_get_data();
	UsbDevice *regs = &REGS->DEVICE;
	uint8_t ep_idx = USB_EP_GET_IDX(ep);
	UsbDeviceEndpoint *endpoint = &regs->DeviceEndpoint[ep_idx];
+	UsbDeviceDescriptor *desc = &data->descriptors[ep_idx];
+	UsbDeviceDescBank *bank;
+	void *buf;

	if (ep_idx >= USB_NUM_ENDPOINTS) {
		LOG_ERR("endpoint index/address out of range");
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	endpoint->EPINTENCLR.reg = USB_DEVICE_EPINTENCLR_TRCPT0
				 | USB_DEVICE_EPINTENCLR_TRCPT1
				 | USB_DEVICE_EPINTENCLR_RXSTP;

+	if (USB_EP_DIR_IS_IN(ep)) {
+		bank = &desc->DeviceDescBank[1];
+	} else {
+		bank = &desc->DeviceDescBank[0];
+	}
+
+	buf = (void *)bank->ADDR.reg;
+	/*
+	 * We free the ep descriptor memory that was
+	 * allocated in usb_dc_ep_configure()
+	 */
+	if (buf != NULL) {
+		k_free(buf);
+		bank->ADDR.reg = (uintptr_t) NULL;
+	}

	return 0;
}
```
